## Summary

All four files have been read completely. Here's the key architecture:

1. **`CustomActionButton.cs`** — Abstract base class for custom ability buttons. Contains `CreateButton()` (instantiates from `HudManager.Instance.AbilityButton`), `ClickHandler()` (manages cooldown/effect/uses logic), and `FixedUpdateHandler()` (called every FixedUpdate to tick timers and update button UI). Also defines `CustomActionButton<T>` for target-based buttons.

2. **`CustomButtonManager.cs`** — Static manager holding all registered buttons in `CustomButtons` list, plus event type dictionaries. `RegisterButton()` creates instances and wires up generic event types.

3. **`HudManagerPatches.cs`** — Harmony patches on `HudManager`. The `StartPostfix` (patching `HudManager.Start`) creates the `BottomLeft` container, instantiates all custom buttons via `button.CreateButton()`, sets up vanilla keybind icons, and triggers `CoResizeUI()`. Also patches `SetHudActive` (visibility) and `Update` (keybind polling).

4. **`PlayerControlPatches.cs`** — The `PlayerControlFixedUpdatePostfix` (patching `PlayerControl.FixedUpdate`) is the per-frame driver: it iterates all `CustomButtonManager.CustomButtons`, checks `button.Enabled(role)`, and calls `button.FixedUpdateHandler(__instance)` for enabled buttons. This is the entry point that makes custom button timers tick.

